    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d603">

                <pb facs="0879" n="865" sameAs="frus:frus1946v06/pg865"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.24/1–247: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1947-01-02T17:00:00+03:00">January 2, 1947—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 2—8:56 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6. I talked with Molotov on lend-lease settlement before receiving <pb
                        facs="0880" n="866" xml:id="pg866"/> your 2198 on pipe line.<anchor n="54"
                        xml:id="d603-f1"/> However he stated that he understood that this was
                    proceeding satisfactorily. His general attitude toward lend-lease settlement was
                    of course noncommittal as it is impossible for Soviet official, even of status
                    of Molotov, to make direct decision in Moscow without other consultations. I
                    presented the US point of view as vigorously as possible and followed my
                    conversation with an <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi><anchor n="55"
                        xml:id="d603-f2"/> confirming my discussion and asking for the earliest
                    possible decision from Soviet Govt. I would hardly expect reply from Molotov
                    before 2 weeks at best but I will follow up my <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi> in about 10 days. We will be alert for any premature news
                        break.<anchor n="56" xml:id="d603-f3"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d603-f1" n="54" xml:id="d603-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed, but see footnote 53, <ref target="#pg865">p. 865</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d603-f2" n="55" xml:id="d603-f2-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d603-f3" n="56" xml:id="d603-f3-content">
                <p>In reply the Department informed the Embassy in telegram 6, January 2, 7 p.m.,
                    that it proposed to tell the press on January 3 that the Embassy had taken up
                    the question of a lend-lease settlement with the Soviet Government, and that no
                    further announcements were contemplated before receipt of the Soviet reply.
                    (861.24/1–247) Concerning the Department’s announcement, see <hi
                        rendition="#italic">New York Times</hi>, January 4, 1947, p. 5, col. 8.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
